"== Japanese Fusion #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A ferocious black and grey Hannya mask rendered with deep smoky shadows while only the horns and one tear are saturated blood red. Dangerous, dramatic, magnetic. In the right panel it is on a man, forearm. CREATE EXCEPTIONAL CONTEMPORARY TATTOO ART. CORE PRINCIPLE: JAPANESE ROOTS. NO CREATIVE CEILING. Japanese tattoo art provides extraordinary lessons in: Movement Body flow Scale Foreground and background Wind Water Cloud Rhythm Repetition Negative space Large compositional relationships USE THOSE PRINCIPLES. DO NOT LET THEM BECOME A CAGE. This collection should explore what happens when Japanese-inspired tattoo composition encounters contemporary tattoo art. Possible collisions include: Japanese and realism Japanese and blackwork Japanese and neo-traditional Japanese and surrealism Japanese and graphic design Japanese and modern illustration Japanese and abstraction Japanese and selective color Japanese and contemporary subject matter Do not randomly combine styles. THE FUSION MUST HAVE A REASON. One visual language should strengthen another. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  look at it: " + $u } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
"== Mixed Media #01 =="
